## Context

Found during ticket 0571's byte-compare invariant (PR #1269). `load_edges()`
in both genealogy renderers returns `list(set(...))`, so edge draw order
follows the Python string-hash seed: two runs of the SAME code on the same
inputs, unpinned, produce different bytes (first difference at byte 3375 of
`fig_genealogy.html`, an edge `<line>`; the PNG differs too). The 0571 A/B
compare only passed with `PYTHONHASHSEED` pinned — an unpinned compare
looked like a regression until the discriminator isolated it.

This contradicts the Phase-2 contract ("fast, deterministic",
`.claude/rules/architecture.md`) and defeats the point of `save_figure()`
stripping metadata for byte-reproducible PNGs: the metadata is stable but
the pixels are not. Any future refactor-validation byte-compare on these
figures will report a spurious diff.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/figures/plot_genealogy.py` — `load_edges()`, `list(set(...))`
- `scripts/figures/plot_genealogy_html.py` — same pattern
- `scripts/pipeline_io.py` — `save_figure()` byte-reproducibility rationale

## Actions

1. Replace the `list(set(...))` dedup with an order-stable one (`sorted(set(...))`
   or `dict.fromkeys`) in both renderers — one shared helper if they can share.
2. Sweep other `plot_*`/`export_*` scripts for the same `list(set(` /
   iteration-over-set pattern feeding an output; fix in the same commit if
   trivial, list here if not.

## Test

Red first: build `fig_genealogy.html` twice under two different
`PYTHONHASHSEED` values and `cmp` — fails today. Green after the stable
ordering. Mark integration (subprocess).

## Verification

- [ ] Two builds under different hash seeds are byte-identical (PNG and HTML)
- [ ] `make lint` and `make check` pass

## Exit criteria

Genealogy figures are byte-reproducible without pinning `PYTHONHASHSEED`,
and no other Phase-2 output script feeds set iteration order into an artifact.